  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern in the short term is a weakening bow-echo \nthat has moved southeast from South Dakota into northern Nebraska. \nWhile the core of this bow and the associated severe risk should stay\nnorth into Nebraska, it is increasingly likely that its outflow \nboundary will push into northeast Kansas during the daytime hours \ntoday. Depending on the strength of this outflow and the amount of \nlingering showers and weak storms behind it, this may help to limit \nafternoon temperatures and heat indices by a few degrees, \nparticularly towards the Nebraska border. It will be hot and humid \nregardless though, particularly south of wherever the outflow \nboundary ends up before washing out. Will keep the current Heat \nAdvisory and Heat Warning in place, as heat indices will most likely \nstill approach 110 along/south of I-70 and 105 north of I-70. A few \nmore showers and storms may redevelop overnight north of the boundary\nnear the Nebraska border, but weak forcing and strong CIN should \nkeep the overall thunderstorm potential low.\n\nThursday, Friday, and Saturday will remain hot. Temperatures will \nlikely remain a few degrees lower along the stationary boundary near \nthe Nebraska border, but most places elsewhere will climb into the \nupper 90s with heat indices in the mid/upper 100s. Low temperatures \nonly in the upper 70s will cause additional heat stress. There will \nbe a continued potential for a few scattered storms to develop along \nand just north of the boundary, most likely towards north-central KS \nThursday and Friday evenings. Inverted-V profiles with modest shear \nsuggest the potential for northeastward moving clusters with \nisolated damaging winds and perhaps some small hail.\n\nWe finally see some improvement by Sunday as the front begins to \npush southward through the area. There is still some uncertainty in \nguidance in exactly how fast it will move through. A slower frontal \nprogression would likely keep temperatures across east-central KS in \nthe upper 90s, while a faster frontal push could keep temperatures \nin the 80s area-wide. Regardless, model spread is lower for Monday, \nwith post-frontal high temperatures mostly back in the 80s. Some \ncontinued low chances for showers and storms will possible along and \nbehind the front, but with little upper support coverage should \nremain scattered.",
